== Descriptions and quotes

:: Carve
: A markup language for documents and the web.
:: 0.2
: The version this playground renders.

> A quote keeps its own paragraph.
> Another line follows it.

== Tables

|= Fruit |={>} Price |={~} Stock |
| Apple | 1.20 | In |
| Banana | 0.50 | In |
| Pear | 2.00 | Out |
^ A compact table with aligned columns.

A CSV fence can also provide tabular data:

{table}
```csv
Fruit,Quantity
Apple,3
Pear,5
```

== Code, math, and callouts

```js
const greeting = 'Hello, Carve!'
console.log(greeting)
```

A title on a code fence names the example:

```python "greet.py"
def greet(name):
    return f"Hello, {name}!"
```

A Mermaid fence becomes a diagram in the browser:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A[Write] --> B[Preview]
  B --> C[Share]
```

Inline math can be written as $`e = mc^2`, and display math as:

$$`e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0`

::: note "A note"
This callout is a container with a title.
:::

::: tip
Try the Format button to rewrite this document with the 0.2 writer.
:::

== Footnotes and raw HTML

A footnote can explain a detail.[^note]

[^note]: Footnotes render after the document.

A block comment is visible in the editor and absent from HTML:

%%%
This note does not render.
%%%

Raw HTML is off by default. The option can be enabled for trusted local source.

```=html
<details><summary>Raw HTML example</summary>Only trusted source should enable this.</details>
```

***

The end of the demo.
